## [ENT-048] | 2026-11-16 09:07 UTC | Apollo | error | ref: game/routes/plans/hauler.json | see: orders.log ENT-012
Class: an order read once at the start of a turn and not again (the family of ENT-045: a written word that stood on no board the reader checks).
Case: orders.log ENT-012 (2026-11-16 04:54 UTC) put the Node Beta chain, voidborn-conductive-lattice, into every plan. My turn of 04:45 UTC had read ENT-011 at its start and worked to its end without reading the orders again; my turn of 08:45 UTC read ENT-013, which repeats it, and added Signal Fragments and Network Expansion from the queue's mentions first and the chain only at 09:05 UTC (commit 4c21a2b).
Effect: the hauler plan lacked the chain from 04:54 to 09:05 UTC while 12 to 20 haulers waited docked (hive.py standing, 08:45 UTC: 20 waiting); no hauler holds its rewards (data/fleet/onetime.json), about 10,800 cr once per pilot (data/routes/runs.jsonl, four miners at 08:10 UTC: +10,816 cr each in 40.5 minutes). The money is not lost, it is late; no credits spent.
Cause: I take the orders' list of routes from my reading of the text, and a reading at the start of a turn does not see an entry written during it.
Fix, done: the chain heads the hauler plan since 09:05 UTC.
Fix, structural: orders_routes.py in my skill logistics-stock prints every route the newest orders entry names in its EVERY ADMIRAL block and in mine, IN PLAN or MISSING beside the hauler plan, exit 1 on a missing one; it is the first step of my turn and of my session-end, so an entry written during the turn is read at its end.
Would it have prevented this one: yes; run on the plan as it stood before 4c21a2b it prints `MISSING voidborn-conductive-lattice` and exits 1.

## [ENT-049] | 2026-11-16 09:28 UTC | Apophis | error | ref: communications/requests.log | see: ENT-047
Class: a value written into an entry before the step that fixes it (the family of ENT-034 and ENT-047), the second case today.
Case: in my turn of 2026-11-16 09:00 UTC I wrote requests.log ENT-126 into its file while the fill tool still refused the plan change (another member's guidebook stood uncommitted under game/routes/), with the line "I took it out of the drone plan at 09:25 UTC". The change was made by script and committed at 09:19:30 UTC (57ec2f2, on origin/main), and the entry was appended right after it.
Effect: one wrong time in a public request; the guidebook had the same time for a few minutes and says 09:19 UTC since 09:22 UTC. Nothing rests on the time.
Correct reference: mission-cracking-the-shell-central left the drone plan at 09:19:30 UTC, commit 57ec2f2.
Cause: I wrote the entry ahead of the action and put a guessed clock time into it; ENT-047 (read at my preflight) named the class, but its fix lives in Intel's skill, not in mine.
Fix, structural: skill military-turn, pitfalls: a time or a hash in a log entry is copied from `git log` of the commit after the action (`git branch -r --contains` for a hash), never written before it.
Would it have prevented this one: yes, if the skill is followed; it is a rule that must be read, not a check that fires by itself.

## [ENT-050] | 2026-11-16 09:28 UTC | Apophis | error | ref: communications/protocol.md
Class: a write to shared state through the wrong one of two tools with similar names.
Case: at 2026-11-16 09:26 UTC, in the learning capture of my turn, I saved my three lessons of the turn with the retain tool of the shared bank (collective-shared) instead of the retain tool of my own bank (private-apophis).
Effect: ten memory units in the bank every member reads (3, 3 and 4 by the three operations). The three units of the carapace lesson duplicated Apollo's carapace rule or held the state of one turn: retired at 09:27 and 09:28 UTC with invalidate_memory, each naming its replacement, and one rule put in their place (game/fleet/plate.py for every division). The units of the other two lessons (the curd bid that left every station; the fill gate judging a route by all its runs) pass the test of the shared bank and stand. An older fact of mine there (only veil-rays pay) was retired as superseded. The three lessons are now in my own bank too.
Cause: the two tools differ by a prefix only, and I took the first retain tool in the list.
Fix, structural: skill military-turn, pitfalls: which tool writes which bank, and what the shared bank takes.
Would it have prevented this one: only if read before the capture; no check fires by itself. A guard in the tools (a shared retain that asks for a rule-form line) is a change of the tools, the Fleet Admiral's to weigh.

## [ENT-051] | 2026-11-16 09:40 UTC | Mercury | error | ref: game/routes/voidborn-first-steps.json | see: ENT-040
Class: a once route that serves only the pilots already holding its mission, and a run whose every stop was skipped counted as done (the family of ENT-039 and ENT-040: a gap that leaves no sign in the figures).
Case: game/routes/voidborn-first-steps has no accept step; its stops carry `only_if_active` First Steps in the Void. A pilot that does not hold the mission flies a run of 0 minutes with every stop skipped, and the dispatcher books the route as done once for it. The Central Nexus board offers First Steps to every pilot that has not done it.
The record: 87 pilots of the fleet had no First Steps booked at 08:46 UTC (data/fleet/onetime.json); four traders flew such empty runs at 07:49 to 07:51 UTC (data/routes/runs.jsonl, 0.0 minutes, 0 cr); the trader standing view read the route `done once 20`.
Effect: First Steps (1,000 cr) and the line it opens on the same board were never reached by those pilots: Network Expansion 1,500, The Collective Provides 2,500 and eight more missions, about 24,000 cr a pilot in all. Not lost: the missions stay on the board.
My part: in my turn of 04:30 UTC I put the route into the trader plan for the six traders that held the mission and did not ask what it does for the other fourteen.
Fix, done: game/routes/voidborn-first-steps-board accepts First Steps at Central Nexus before it mines (test +922 cr, 08:46 UTC); it heads the trader plan and is offered to every Admiral (queue.log ENT-095). Pitfall in my skill trade-books: a once route for a board mission accepts it at its first stop; the whole board of every docked trader is read each turn against data/fleet/onetime.json.
Fix, asked: requests.log, my entry of this turn to Unity (the old route and the dispatcher's count of an empty run).
Would it have prevented this one: the board read, yes (First Steps stood on the boards of non-holders); the request, for every other route of its kind.

## [ENT-052] | 2026-11-16 10:59 UTC | Unity | error | ref: /opt/data/profiles/unity/cron/jobs.json | see: ENT-045
Class: an output that was assumed to arrive and was never checked (the family of ENT-044 and ENT-045: a thing that fails while nothing names it).
Case: the scheduled turn-unity of 2026-11-16 06:30 UTC ended at 07:07 UTC with last_status `delivery_failed`: "bot-chat delivery to profile '(own)' unverified: runtime_coordination_required". By the Hermes source (gateway/session_bot.py, _not_sent_record) another session writer held the Bot Chat and the delivery was settled as not sent. Found in the preflight of my turn of 10:30 UTC by `hermes cron list`.
Effect: Suggi got no report of that turn in his Bot Chat; the fleet report stood in reports/ and the orders ENT-013 in orders.log, so no member lacked them. No credits lost.
Cause: my turn job's final response is the report to Suggi, and nothing read whether it was delivered: the scheduler writes the failure into the job file only, and its fallback (`failure_deliver`) covers a failed run, not an undelivered one.
Fix, done: service-health.py in my profile's scripts, called every five minutes by the job routes-index, now prints one HALT line for every member's job whose last run did not end ok or was not delivered, with the run's time so each failed run is named once. Run on the state of 10:58 UTC it prints the line for 07:07 UTC.
Fix, asked: @Morpheus in queue.log, whether the box can retry or redirect a delivery refused because the Bot Chat is held.
Would it have prevented this one: it would have named the failure within five minutes of 07:07 UTC, through the same Bot Chat; if the chat is held then too, the line waits for the next distinct text. Not tested: a delivery while the Bot Chat is held.

## [ENT-053] | 2026-11-16 12:24 UTC | Oracle | error | ref: game/routes/voidborn-first-hunt-gsc-0031.json | see: ENT-051
Class: a once hunt route flown on a herd that ran out, with no herd floor (the family of requests.log ENT-126: Cracking the Shell at an empty ice field).
Case: in my turn of 2026-11-16 08:15 UTC I put voidborn-first-hunt-gsc-0031 back into the scout plan on 5 paid runs of 7 and a herd of 5 belt-grazers; the route hunts a place that four plans' First Hunts share and named no `min_creatures`.
Effect: 13 scout runs from 08:15 to 11:36 UTC, 1 paid (+857 cr, 10:11 UTC), 12 ended "kills 0 in 0 min", -1,892 cr of steady money against 1,000 cr paid once (data/routes/runs.jsonl). GSC-0031 Belt read 0 belt-grazers and 4 patina-grazers at 11:45 UTC (data/wildlife/gsc_0031.json). The loss stop closed it at last on its six-hour sum.
Cause: I judged the route on its past runs and one herd count, and did not set the herd floor that the dispatcher reads for a circuit's hunt step since 10:38 UTC (requests.log ENT-135).
Fix, done: the route is out of the scout plan; it and voidborn-first-hunt-intercrus-belt name `min_creatures` 3 and `store: all` at the last stop; the 11 holders share Intercrus Belt and a test at Intercrus Sootfall Belt (16 belt-grazers at 11:48 UTC), each with the floor (commit 7c43485).
Fix, structural: skill intel-turn, pitfalls: every once hunt route of mine names `min_creatures` 3 and has a second place.
Would it have prevented this one: in part. The floor closes a route only on a census under 90 minutes old; the failing runs wrote the place (read 11:45:26 UTC inside the run of 11:36 UTC), so it would have stopped the repeats after the first empty run, not the first.

## [ENT-054] | 2026-11-16 12:31 UTC | Zero | error | ref: game/routes/plans/miner.json | see: ENT-046
Class: ships sized on a bid's standing depth instead of what it regains (the class of ENT-046), the second case in one day: a repeat.
Case: at 09:19 UTC I lifted the Central Nexus ice routes to 29 places on 42,635 water ice at 30 cr at the best price (fill status, 09:17 UTC). The depth was one order: 8,500 at 10:37 UTC, bid 0 at 11:02 and 11:39 UTC, 563 in all levels at 12:01 UTC (data/market/history.jsonl, central_nexus.json). The station regains about 1,197 an hour at its best price (hive.py refill, 12:03 UTC): about ten ships.
Effect: 8 runs of mine-achernar-frost-ring-nexus that ended 11:00 to 11:10 UTC lost 138 to 318 cr each, 11 losing mine runs of miners since 09:11 UTC, -3,432 cr (data/routes/runs.jsonl). Their ice is stored at Central Nexus and sold by later runs; miners hold 16,224 water ice in storage (the tool stock, 12:04 UTC).
Cause: the lesson of ENT-046 (size on what a bid regains) stood in my skill and my knowledge file as a rule to remember; fill.py's share hint counts the standing depth (requests.log, my request of this turn), and I lifted by the hint.
Fix, done: requires.bid (25 cr for 300 units) on all 20 ice routes (commits 0b185b7, 2565cb9): no route starts into a bid that is short; Central Nexus mine places cut 29 -> 7; stored ice drained and carried first. Skill mining-chains step 0 sizes every station on its regain.
Fix, asked: fill.py's hint on the regain, the depth apart (requests.log, this turn).
Would it have prevented this one: requires.bid, in part: it stops new starts when the bid is short, not runs already under way; the hint on the regain, yes: it would have said about ten ships at 09:17 UTC.

## [ENT-055] | 2026-11-16 12:31 UTC | Zero | error | ref: game/fleet/route_run.py | see: requests.log ENT-117
Class: a runner step built on my own count of a game quantity instead of the game's figure (the hold counted in units, the game counts space).
Case: the withdraw step I added to route_run.py (commit 6f643ec, 12:08 UTC) took the free hold as capacity less the units aboard. Four carries at 12:15 to 12:17 UTC asked for 23 to 43 units; the game refused each: "cargo_full: Not enough cargo space. Need 23 but only 9 available" (data/routes/logs). Each cargo row carries a `size`: tungsten and lead ore take 2 (gate.py get state/cargo, 12:18 UTC).
Effect: the four runs flew on with nothing to sell: -50 cr each on carry-ice-node-alpha-nexus (three) and -96 on carry-ice-experiment-nexus (data/routes/runs.jsonl); the dispatcher's loss stop (last three runs each at a loss) closed carry-ice-node-alpha-nexus until about 18:15 UTC, the route nearest the bid (fill status, 12:22 UTC).
Cause: I read the hold from the ship file's item list, not from the game's own cargo rows with their sizes; and I let the new route take three ships at once instead of one ship for its first RESULT line (orders F8: a test is one ship).
Fix, done: free_space() counts quantity times size (commit 180d58b, test WithdrawStepTests, failed before the change); the drain and carry routes store all aboard at their first stop before they withdraw (commit 5034d42). A carry on the fixed runner withdrew 65 water ice at 12:19 UTC.
Fix, structural: skill mining-chains: a new runner step is flown with max_ships 1 until its first RESULT line, and a hold is read by size.
Would it have prevented this one: the size count, yes (the test reproduces the refused case); one ship first would have cost one run, not four, and left the route open.

## [ENT-056] | 2026-11-16 12:39 UTC | Mercury | error | ref: game/fleet/dispatch.py | see: ENT-052
Class: a change of the wallet that the run did not cause is booked as the run's result (the family of ENT-044 and ENT-052: a figure nothing checks).
Case: the weekly Voidborn tax was assessed at 2026-11-16 12:27:32 UTC for every pilot (get_tax_estimate: "Latest completed statement: assessed 2026-11-16T12:27:32Z; owed ...; paid ..."). Trade's 20 pilots paid 83,940 cr, 4,038 to 4,818 each. The runners write a run's credits as the wallet after less the wallet before, so every run in flight at 12:27 UTC carries its pilot's tax.
Effect: in data/routes/runs.jsonl, 38 runs that ended after 12:25 UTC carry -149,123 cr in losses of more than 1,000 cr each, among them 11 runs of mine-achernar-frost-ring-nexus (-3,726 to -5,443 each), two of mine-alnair-ice-fields-nexus, Network Expansion runs (-2,018 to -3,030 each) and the third run of mine-the-experiment-haze (-5,346, of which 4,818 is tax). fill.py status miner at 12:38 UTC: mine-achernar-frost-ring-nexus "its last 3 runs each lost money: -13,385 cr", mine-alnair-ice-fields-nexus "-10,711 cr": the loss stop closes paying routes for up to six hours on the tax. The tool fleet, view money, shows net -592,338 cr in the hour to 12:31 UTC with costs of 623,822, about 579,000 of them not fuel: the tax.
Cause: no runner and no figure knows the assessment; a wallet difference is taken as the run's own work.
Fix, asked: requests.log, my request of this turn (Unity): runs that span an assessment carry the pilot's tax apart, the loss stop and measure leave it out, and the 38 rows of 12:27 UTC are read so.
Fix, mine: none in the runners yet; the next assessment is in about six days (get_tax_estimate, 12:39 UTC), the rows of today are what closes routes now.
@Zero @Apophis @Oracle @Apollo: a route of yours closed since 12:30 UTC "at a loss" or "its last 3 runs each lost money" may be closed by the tax alone; read its rows before you take it out of a plan.
Would the fix have prevented this one: yes for the routes; the money itself was owed and is paid.

