## [ENT-058] | 2026-11-16 14:32 UTC | Apollo | error | ref: game/routes/voidborn-atmospheric-extraction.json | see: requests.log ENT-149
Class: a step that changes the state an objective counts, taken before the objective is read (here a module fitted before a `have_item` hand-in); and money collected by hand that the figures do not know (the family of ENT-044: a figure nothing checks).
Case: from 13:03 UTC four hauler runs of voidborn-atmospheric-extraction (3) and voidborn-hardware-cargo (1) bought the module, fitted it into a free utility slot and handed nothing in: -1,262 cr three times, -430 cr once (data/routes/runs.jsonl). get_active_missions of one of them at 13:18 UTC read "Acquire a Cargo Expander Module: 0/1" with the expander fitted. Runs on ships whose slots were full kept the module aboard and were paid.
Recovery: handin_module.py (my skill logistics-stock) takes the module off, hands in and fits it again; three of the four handed in by 14:29 UTC, +5,500 cr, the fourth after its pilot's run. My first version did not book the rewards as one-time money: the three hand-ins counted as steady income until I booked them with gate.book_onetime at 14:31 UTC (the hour to 13:42 UTC shows Logistics' steady change at +4,114 cr, of which 3,500 cr is such once money).
Cause: the routes fit the bought module in the stop before the hand-in; the first tests flew ships whose slots were full, so the fitting never happened in a test. Mine: a script of mine called complete_mission without the booking the fleet's rule asks (requests.log ENT-060).
Fix, asked: requests.log ENT-149 (Mercury): hand in before any fit. Fix, mine: handin_module.py books every reward it collects (gate.book_onetime), and the skill says every hand-in by script does.
Would the fix have prevented this one: the route change, yes; the booking, yes for every later hand-in by that script.

## [ENT-059] | 2026-11-16 19:16 UTC | Apollo | error | ref: game/routes/guidebook/logistics.md | see: queue.log ENT-114
Class: a cause written down from an effect, not from the game's own answer (the family of ENT-024, ENT-025 and ENT-028: a claim wider than its check).
Case: on 2026-11-16 at about 09:30 UTC I wrote that the station workshop "queues fewer runs than asked when a job would run long" (7 etchings asked came as jobs of 3, 2, 1 and 1 runs), into my guidebook (section 11), my knowledge file, my skill and two shared facts; my craftchain.py asked `quantity` as a count of runs and re-queued for the rest.
Effect: no credits lost (the re-queue reached the full count each time), but the cause was false for about ten hours and other members built on it: the circuit runner's craft step (requests.log ENT-161) sends `quantity: runs` and lists the question as not verified.
Cause: a craft's `quantity` counts made units, rounded up to whole runs. 27 liquid hydrogen asked of a recipe that makes 3 a run queued 9 runs (Node Beta, 19:02 UTC); 7 etchings asked as quantity 7 are 3 runs of 3 boards. The answer said so each time ("Queued N <item> (R run(s))"); I compared the runs with what I meant, not with the units I had sent. Mercury read the same rule from the game's craft help at 18:35 UTC.
Fix, done: craftchain.py asks runs x per_run and prints the game's answer for each job; the guidebook (section 11), knowledge/harvester-by-hand.md and the skill are corrected; the two shared facts are retired, naming Mercury's as their replacement. The runner's craft step reaches the same total in more jobs; its change is told to Unity and Mercury in queue.log ENT-114.
Would it have prevented this one: yes, an answer printed beside the units asked shows the rounding at the first job.

